course: everyday
lesson: 386
title: Lily, the calm «princess»
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lily always has a neat ponytail, tidy books and a pencil case where every pen points the same way, so the boys call her «princess». She rolls her eyes and says: «Yes, and princesses can be scientists.» That spring her volcano project won the science fair, and the boys who had laughed queued up to see it fizz.
explain: One wet lunchtime a fight broke out at the back of the classroom. Chairs scraped, everybody jumped up to watch and some even shouted «go on!». Lily's heart was racing too, but she kept her face calm, slipped out of the door and fetched Mr Hughes from the staffroom. She did not cheer, and she did not just stand and watch. By the time the others noticed she was gone, the teacher was already there.
explain: Lily's calm is not the same as being weak or stuck up. The boys thought she was proud because she was quiet, until she helped one of them with fractions and they found out she was simply shy. In PE a ball hit her nose and it bled all down her T-shirt; she pressed it, leaned forward and did not cry, and nobody called her «delicate» after that.
explain: Another time, when Lily's friends cheered at a playground fight, Lily stayed quiet and would not join in. One friend stopped cheering with her, and the next time two more did. Lily also asked Maddie to teach her to climb trees: she got stuck on the first branch, laughed at herself, and Maddie's mum helped her down. And when a scared new girl arrived, the teacher chose Lily to show her around, because Lily's calm made people feel safe. The two became friends.
explain: Her gran told her that real princesses and queens in history often had to be very brave, and Lily did her history project on one. Her mum used to say «be ladylike» when Lily ran in the playground, until Lily asked what «ladylike» means. Mum thought and said «kind and confident». «Then I can be that and run,» said Lily.
fact: Being tidy, quiet or calm says nothing about whether a person is weak.
fact: Quiet people are often shy, not proud or «stuck up».
fact: Staying calm in a crisis lets you do something useful, such as fetching a teacher.
fact: When a fight starts, the most helpful thing a bystander can do is get an adult, not watch or cheer.
fact: Cheering at a fight makes it bigger and can get someone badly hurt.
fact: When one person refuses to cheer, others often find the courage to stop too.
fact: For a nosebleed, sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for about ten minutes.
fact: Feeling your heart race is normal; calm people feel fear too, they just choose what to do next.
fact: Trying something new that you are not good at, like climbing a tree, takes courage.
fact: Asking what a word really means can change how people use it.
exercise: Why did the boys call Lily «princess»? | Because her hair and books were always neat | Because she lived in a big house like a castle | Because she was the tallest girl | Because she won the science fair
exercise: How did Lily answer being called «princess»? | «Yes, and princesses can be scientists» | She cried and went home early | She pushed the boy over in the line for that | She called him a name back
exercise: When the fight broke out in the classroom, what did Lily feel? | Her heart raced, but she kept calm | Nothing at all, she was just bored by it all | Excited, like at a big football match | So scared that she hid under her desk
exercise: What did Lily do during the classroom fight? | Fetched the teacher from the staffroom | Cheered loudly for the smaller boy | Joined in to pull the two of them apart herself | Filmed it to show the head
exercise: Why is fetching an adult wiser than watching a fight? | An adult can stop it before someone is badly hurt | Watching a fight is against the law for children | The fighters want to be left alone | Adults like to watch fights too
exercise: Lily's friends cheered at a fight, but Lily would not. What happened next? | One friend joined her, and next time two more did | Her friends stopped talking to her for ever after that | The fighters got cross with her | The teacher told her off for not cheering
exercise: Why did the boys think Lily was «stuck up»? | Because she was quiet | Because she was rude to them | Because she had a new phone | Because she never shared sweets
exercise: What did the boys find out when Lily helped with maths? | She was shy, not proud | She was secretly very bossy | She did not really know maths | She only helped to show off
exercise: Lily asked Maddie to teach her to climb trees. How did that go? | She got stuck, laughed, and Maddie's mum helped her down | She climbed higher than Maddie on her very first go up | She refused to try once she saw the tree | Maddie laughed at her and walked off
exercise: What did Lily's mum decide «ladylike» means? | Kind and confident | Never running or getting muddy | Always wearing a dress | Speaking only when spoken to
exercise: Why was Lily chosen to show the new girl around? | Because she was calm and made people feel safe | Because she was the teacher's niece and got picked first | Because nobody else wanted to | Because she was the best at sport
exercise: What did Lily's gran tell her about princesses in history? | Many of them had to be very brave | They never did any work at all, ever | They were only ever in fairy tales | They were never allowed to learn anything
exercise: A ball hit Lily's nose in PE and it bled. What is right for a nosebleed? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Tip your head right back and look up at the ceiling | Lie flat on the floor | Blow your nose hard to clear it
exercise: After the nosebleed, why did the boys stop calling Lily «delicate»? | She stayed steady and did not cry | She hit the boy who threw the ball | She told them she was a black belt | She never went to PE again
exercise: Which is another kind of courage Lily showed? | Refusing to cheer when all her friends did | Jumping off the top of the climbing frame | Shouting at the teacher in front of everyone | Starting a fight to look tough
test: «lol princess cant even climb a tree». What is true about Lily? | She tried, got stuck and laughed, which took courage | She never tried because she was scared of falling | She climbed it faster than anyone in her class had | She cried until the boys helped
test: Big fight at the back, whole class up on chairs yelling. Your best move? | Go and get a teacher straight away | Get closer so you can see | Shout for your mate to win | Start filming it for the group chat
test: «she's well stuck up, never says a word to us». Which is more likely? | She's shy and needs a chance | She thinks she's better than everyone | She can't speak any English yet | She's planning something sneaky
test: Kid in PE: «ur nose is pouring, tip ur head back!» What should Lily do? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part | Tip her head right back, like she was told | Lie down on the mat | Keep playing and ignore it till it stops
test: «if u don't cheer ur not one of us». Lily stays quiet. Is she wrong? | No, refusing to cheer at a fight is brave | Yes, she should always cheer along with her friends | Yes, cheering doesn't change anything | No, but only because she's a girl
test: «calm people don't get scared, they're just weird». True? | No, they feel fear but choose what to do | Yes, calm people don't feel anything at all | Yes, calm people are just slow | No, calm people are only pretending to be
test: Mum: «Run like that and you're not very ladylike.» What did Lily do that was wise? | Asked what «ladylike» really means | Stopped running for ever, even in PE | Shouted that Mum was wrong about everything | Ran off and hid in the toilets
test: New girl's first day, scared stiff. What did Lily's calm give her? | It made her feel safe, and they became friends | Nothing, because calm people are just boring | It made her more nervous | It made her think Lily was stuck up and posh
test: «a princess? she's soft, look at her neat hair». What does the lesson teach? | You can't tell how tough someone is by looks | Neat people are always soft inside | Messy people are always the tough ones | Hair shows how brave you are
test: Teacher: «Lily, why didn't you stay and watch like everybody else?» Best answer? | «I went to get help so nobody got hurt» | «I didn't want to get my nice dress dirty» | «I was going to cheer later» | «Fights are for boys, not me»
exam: Identify the conduct most appropriate for a pupil present at the commencement of a physical altercation in a classroom. | The summoning of a member of staff without delay | The close observation of the altercation | The vocal encouragement of one participant | The recording of the altercation upon a device
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between a reserved disposition and arrogance? | A reserved disposition frequently arises from shyness and does not indicate arrogance | A reserved disposition invariably indicates arrogance towards one's peers | Reserved persons are incapable of courage | Arrogance is the sole cause of reticence in children
exam: Select the response consistent with established first-aid guidance in respect of an epistaxis (nosebleed) sustained during physical education. | The head is inclined forward and the soft portion of the nose is compressed | The head is tilted backwards so as to arrest the flow of blood | The pupil is placed supine upon the floor | The nose is blown forcefully to clear it
exam: It is given that a pupil declines to applaud an altercation notwithstanding the encouragement of her companions. State the consequence most frequently observed. | Others subsequently find the resolve to refrain likewise | The altercation is invariably prolonged | The pupil is permanently excluded by her peers from all games | The participants direct their aggression towards her
